@@ -0,0 +1,33 @@
+# BIMCraft security changes — local review draft
+
+Base: IPedrax/BIMCraft commit 818967487278dca6ae68e8e1861fc4250e41727a.
+Branch: security/harden-untrusted-input. No remote push or upstream modification.
+
+## Implemented
+
+- Desktop updater is notification-only while publisher_trust_configured() is false. No publisher key is invented or fetched from the potentially compromised release source. A release hash and SHA256SUMS from the same source are integrity checks, not independent publisher authentication. This is containment, not a completed signature-verification system.
+- Windows --finish-update entry and helper-copy launch fail closed without publisher trust. The entry also requires configured Windows paths. Removed the retry that released the MSI deny-write/delete handle; installer launch failure and unsuccessful MSI exit codes now report failure. Parent-pipe read errors abort processing. Low-level installer routines remain for tests; they are not an authenticated updater API.
+- MCP stdio frames capped at 1 MiB before unbounded line allocation, including newline; oversized streams close with InvalidData. Direct handle_line calls enforce the same cap before parsing. Batches capped at 64 requests and scripts at 256 physical lines.
+- MCP denies open/save and allows only explicitly reviewed in-memory command IDs; engine aliases resolve before authorization. Scripts are preflighted in full before executing any command, including parsing schedule parameters to deny CSV path exports through every route. Unknown future commands fail closed. File import/export tools are removed from discovery and denied on direct calls. This deliberately removes MCP file operations rather than implementing a race-prone path-prefix sandbox. Local CLI and desktop retain file access.
+- Native project and IFC open use a regular-file handle and a bounded streaming reader, so growth after metadata inspection cannot cause an unbounded read. Direct Model::read and IFC parser inputs have the same 256 MiB ceiling. Other loaders are not comprehensively hardened by this change.
+- Cargo.lock updates rustls-webpki 0.103.15 -> 0.103.16 (RUSTSEC-2026-0382) and webpki-roots 1.0.9 -> 1.0.10 (RUSTSEC-2026-0381). Registry checksums and unchanged non-development dependency requirements were obtained from crates.io. Cargo did not regenerate or validate the lockfile; build compatibility remains unverified.
+- Added MCP denial/limit regression tests, bounded-reader regression test, publisher-trust default test, updated Windows helper expectations, and cargo-fuzz scaffolding for project JSON, IFC, CFB containers and gzip inflation. Existing CI now includes cargo audit.
+
+## Validation actually completed
+
+- Repository cloned and targeted review of updater, MCP, core project/IFC entry points, Revit limit/decompression design, lockfile and CI performed. This is not a complete review of all repository code.
+- git diff --check passes for the local patch.
+- Queried OSV querybatch for all 451 registry package/version entries. Initial scan returned two TLS security advisories and four unmaintained-package advisories. A second live scan after the lock changes returned only the four maintenance advisories listed below. Results are saved beside this report. OSV is supplementary and does not substitute for cargo audit or reachability analysis.
+- cargo fmt --check, cargo test --workspace, cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings, and cargo audit were all attempted. Every command failed to launch with Access is denied (os error 5). Direct toolchain invocation failed too. No Rust compilation, tests, lint, formatting check, cargo audit or fuzz run succeeded. Changes must be validated on a functioning Rust host before use.
+
+## Remaining and unverifiable
+
+1. Implement independent publisher signing and verification: provision a maintainer-controlled public key through a trusted channel; sign a canonical version/target/size/digest manifest; verify before downloading/installing; test wrong keys, corruption, substitution, rollback and key rotation. Only then consider enabling installation. Windows helper launch still needs an authenticated, race-resistant handoff and OS-derived trusted paths; environment paths alone are not sufficient. It remains disabled.
+2. Maintenance advisories remain: ttf-parser 0.25.1 (RUSTSEC-2026-0192), zbus_names 4.3.4 (RUSTSEC-2026-0371), zvariant 5.15.0 (RUSTSEC-2026-0370), zvariant_derive 5.15.0 (RUSTSEC-2026-0372). No fixed versions are listed in those advisories. Trace and migrate their parent dependencies, then test on all platforms. They are not suppressed in CI.
+3. Run the full CI matrix and fuzz targets. Verify lockfile resolution, manually format any failures, exercise actual Windows MSI locking and cancellation, and test actual packaged desktop updates remaining notification-only.
+4. Input byte limits do not bound total model size, render output, CPU cost, recursion in all importers, cumulative decompression, or process lifetime. Full import fuzzing, corpus regression testing and OS resource isolation remain necessary. Existing Revit per-file/per-stream/inflate limits were inspected; no global memory-safety or denial-of-service certification is claimed.
+5. The referenced prior audit could not be recovered: read_thread returned message-reference placeholders, not the audit body. Findings were reconstructed from the stated priorities and current source, so an exact reconciliation with every earlier finding is impossible.
+
+A dedicated patched-windows.yml workflow now runs formatting, linting, full tests, release compilation and version smoke checks before uploading portable Windows executables. It has not run yet; GitHub sign-in/fork creation is pending.
+
+This patch is an uncompiled local review draft, not a complete security certification or a claim that all security issues are fixed.
